{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01GrRWBPTixVrRxyX5f31WWy","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012eHn7ZrgcABENcUqjS9VSQ","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are **Reviewer 1 (R1)** for a top ML venue peer review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject … 6=Strong Accept). Your focus areas are **methodology and experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"Checkpoint Selection Matters More Than Mining Strategy: A Negative Result Study on Hard Negative Mining for Code Retrieval\"\n\n**Core Claims**:\n1. No mining strategy reliably outperforms random sampling at training scales of 10K+ instances, across 4 strategies × 3 seeds × 3 encoders.\n2. Checkpoint selection criterion (best-by-R@1 vs best-by-val-loss) causes 10–18pp variation — 30× larger than mining strategy effect.\n3. Mining effectiveness is inversely proportional to corpus size (scaling law crossing zero at ~4K instances).\n4. Reproduction of CoCoHaNeRe (+3.5pp→+0.2pp) and ANCE (+2.8pp→+0.4pp) fails under controlled conditions.\n5. Proposed minimum evaluation protocol: ≥5 seeds, dual checkpoint criteria, multi-pool evaluation.\n\n**Methodology**:\n- Task: file-level code retrieval (buggy file → file requiring modification), from SWE-smith dataset (11,198 instances, 5,063 repos)\n- 4 strategies: Random (k=7 uniform), Embedding-distance (cosine NN from pretrained encoder, static), Dataflow-aware (CodeQL 3-hop, structurally disconnected ranked by BM25, 66% fallback to BM25-only), BM25 (top-k BM25 non-gold)\n- 3 encoders: Qwen2.5-Coder-3B (decoder-only, pos_sim=0.9855), StarCoder2-3B (decoder-only, pos_sim=0.7985), UniXcoder-base (encoder-only, 125M)\n- Training: LoRA rank=16, InfoNCE τ=0.07, batch 32, pool=8, up to 3 epochs, early stopping by val R@1, seeds {42,43,44}\n- CodeQL pipeline: 0% timeout, 78% connected-not-gold rate, 123/131 repo coverage\n\n**Key Tables**:\n\nTable 1 (Qwen R@1, pool=8, 3 seeds):\n| Strategy | Mean±Std | Δ vs Random | 95% CI |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Random | 0.570±0.027 | — | — |\n| Embedding | 0.568±0.023 | −0.17pp | [−0.92, +0.58] |\n| Dataflow | 0.560±0.014 | −0.99pp | [−2.36, +0.58] |\n| BM25 | 0.570±0.023 | +0.03pp | [−0.55, +0.59] |\n\nTable 2 (Paired within-seed deltas, Qwen):\n| Comparison | s42 | s43 | s44 | Mean | 95% CI |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| embed−random | −0.37 | +0.52 | −0.67 | −0.17 | [−0.92, +0.58] |\n| dataflow−random | −0.47 | +0.10 | −2.59 | −0.99 | [−2.36, +0.58] |\n| bm25−random | +0.23 | +0.26 | −0.39 | +0.03 | [−0.55, +0.59] |\n\nTable 3 (StarCoder2-3B, pool=8, 3 seeds):\n| Strategy | Mean±Std | Δ vs Random | 95% CI |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Random | 0.480±0.017 | — | — |\n| Embedding | 0.476±0.017 | −0.37pp | [−0.52, −0.22] |\n| Dataflow | 0.471±0.016 | −0.86pp | [−1.02, −0.70] |\nNote: Per-encoder CIs EXCLUDE zero for StarCoder (mining hurts).\n\nTable 4 (UniXcoder-base 125M, pool=8, 3 seeds):\n| Strategy | Mean±Std | Δ vs Random | 95% CI |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Random | 0.564±0.012 | — | — |\n| Embedding | 0.560±0.012 | −0.39pp | [−0.85, +0.07] |\n| Dataflow | 0.561±0.012 | −0.22pp | — |\n\nTable 5 (Data scaling, embed−random, Qwen):\n| Data Size | Δ R@1 (pp) | 95% CI | Random R@1 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1K | +1.2 | [+0.1, +2.3] | 0.48 |\n| 3K | +0.5 | [−0.4, +1.4] | 0.52 |\n| 5K | +0.2 | [−0.7, +1.1] | 0.54 |\n| 11K | −0.17 | [−0.92, +0.58] | 0.570 |\n\nTable 6 (Pool size sensitivity, Qwen):\n| Pool | Random | Embed | Dataflow | Embed−Rand |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 8 | 0.570 | 0.568 | 0.560 | −0.17pp |\n| 50 | 0.420 | 0.415 | 0.408 | −0.5pp |\n| 100 | 0.310 | 0.308 | 0.303 | −0.2pp |\n| 200 | 0.210 | 0.208 | 0.205 | −0.2pp |\n| 500 | 0.115 | 0.113 | 0.111 | −0.2pp |\n\nTable 7 (RepoBench-R, Qwen, 3 seeds):\n| Strategy | Easy (pool~6) | Hard (pool~18) | Δ |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Random | 0.875±0.015 | 0.510±0.020 | 36.5pp |\n| Embedding | 0.870±0.014 | 0.507±0.019 | 36.3pp |\n| Dataflow | 0.868±0.016 | 0.503±0.021 | 36.5pp |\n\nTable 8 (Checkpoint criterion divergence): Shows R@1_best vs R@1_val_loss for 8 runs; 67% show divergence, gap up to 1.97pp, 3 ranking flips.\n\nTable 9 (Analyst flexibility decomposition):\n| Source | Variance (pp) | % |\n|---|---|---|\n| Pool size | 6.7 | 44% |\n| Seed selection | 5.0 | 33% |\n| Checkpoint criterion | 3.5 | 23% |\n| **Total** | **15.2** | **100%** |\n| Mining strategy | 0.5 | 3.3% |\n\nTable 10 (Power analysis): 3 seeds → detectable effect 2.5pp; 10 seeds → 0.8pp; 50 seeds → 0.3pp.\n\nTable 11 (Reproduction): CoCoHaNeRe +3.5→+0.2pp (CI [−0.8,+1.2]), ANCE +2.8→+0.4pp (CI [−0.5,+1.3]).\n\nTable 12 (Temperature ablation): τ∈{0.05,0.07,0.20}, all CIs include zero.\n\nTable 13 (Stratified by repo size): All strata CIs include zero.\n\nSection 4.11 additional ablations: Dynamic vs static mining (0.567 vs 0.568), hop depth 1-3 (<0.2pp), distance weighting (0.558 vs 0.560), fusion head (0.571 vs 0.569), failure analysis (8 help, 32 irrelevant, 10 hurt out of 50).\n\n**Experiment Design Context** (full planned experiments — check paper coverage):\nThe full experiment plan included 20+ experiments. Key ones that should appear:\n- M1 CodeQL extraction (covered in Sec 3.3)\n- M2 Qwen 4-strategy × 3-seed (Table 1-2)\n- SWE-bench evaluation (DROPPED — R@1=0.04 vs BM25=0.2733, OOD failure, not mentioned in paper)\n- RepoBench-R (Table 7)\n- Stratified analysis across 5 dimensions (Table 13 only shows repo size)\n- Hop depth ablation (Sec 4.11)\n- Data scaling 1K/3K/5K/11K (Table 5)\n- Fusion head (Sec 4.11)\n- Dataflow distance weighting (Sec 4.11)\n- Failure analysis 50 cases (Sec 4.11)\n- Qualitative examples 5-10 cases (NOT in paper)\n- StarCoder 3-strategy × 3-seed (Table 3, but only shows random/embed/dataflow — no BM25 for StarCoder)\n- Temperature ablation (Table 12, reduced to single seed s42)\n- Dynamic embedding mining (Sec 4.11)\n- BM25 baseline (Table 1)\n- Pool size sensitivity (Table 6)\n- UniXcoder 3-seed (Table 4, but missing CI for dataflow)\n- Extra seeds 5-10 (mentioned in Sec 4.7 — 10-seed result)\n- Published reproduction (Table 11)\n- Checkpoint criterion analysis (Tables 8, 9)\n\nNote: Many results in the paper marked with * are predicted/imagined rather than actually run. The context file reveals that for Qwen, only 6 of 12 runs were completed (random ×3, embed s43/s44, dataflow s43); for StarCoder, only 4 of 9 (random ×3, embed s42). UniXcoder only has s42 actual data.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nWrite a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, with specific references to tables/sections)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, with specific references — focus on methodology and experimental rigor)\n4. **Questions for Authors** (numbered)\n5. **Missing Experiments** (compared to what would be expected for a top venue)\n6. **Minor Issues**\n7. **Overall Assessment** with scores:\n   - Soundness (1-5)\n   - Presentation (1-5)\n   - Contribution (1-5)\n   - Overall (1-6, where 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept)\n   - Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous. For a negative result paper, the bar for methodological soundness is HIGHER than for a positive result paper — the community must trust that the null is real, not an artifact of flawed methodology.\n\nKey things to scrutinize:\n- Are 3 seeds sufficient to claim null? (Paper's own power analysis says 50 seeds needed for 0.3pp)\n- Is pool=8 an appropriate evaluation setting? (Paper criticizes others for small pools but uses pool=8 as primary)\n- Dataflow strategy has 66% fallback — is this a fair comparison?\n- Bootstrap CI methodology — cluster bootstrap or naive?\n- How many results are actually run vs predicted?\n- The \"analyst flexibility\" framing — is 15.2pp additive or is that misleading?\n- Reproduction claims (Table 11) — are the settings truly comparable to original papers?\n- Missing: no figures at all in the paper, only tables\n- Training only uses LoRA rank=16 — would full fine-tuning show different patterns?\n\nOutput your complete review in structured markdown."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1488,"cache_read_input_tokens":73138,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1488},"output_tokens":55,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"163407e6-eb14-494f-b2b9-ca41e03f7d7e","uuid":"b048a606-7ec4-4127-9f69-4d3b7dd1e265"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"ad67ba16d2e04cb3c","tool_use_id":"toolu_012eHn7ZrgcABENcUqjS9VSQ","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are **Reviewer 1 (R1)** for a top ML venue peer review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject … 6=Strong Accept). Your focus areas are **methodology and experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"Checkpoint Selection Matters More Than Mining Strategy: A Negative Result Study on Hard Negative Mining for Code Retrieval\"\n\n**Core Claims**:\n1. No mining strategy reliably outperforms random sampling at training scales of 10K+ instances, across 4 strategies × 3 seeds × 3 encoders.\n2. Checkpoint selection criterion (best-by-R@1 vs best-by-val-loss) causes 10–18pp variation — 30× larger than mining strategy effect.\n3. Mining effectiveness is inversely proportional to corpus size (scaling law crossing zero at ~4K instances).\n4. Reproduction of CoCoHaNeRe (+3.5pp→+0.2pp) and ANCE (+2.8pp→+0.4pp) fails under controlled conditions.\n5. Proposed minimum evaluation protocol: ≥5 seeds, dual checkpoint criteria, multi-pool evaluation.\n\n**Methodology**:\n- Task: file-level code retrieval (buggy file → file requiring modification), from SWE-smith dataset (11,198 instances, 5,063 repos)\n- 4 strategies: Random (k=7 uniform), Embedding-distance (cosine NN from pretrained encoder, static), Dataflow-aware (CodeQL 3-hop, structurally disconnected ranked by BM25, 66% fallback to BM25-only), BM25 (top-k BM25 non-gold)\n- 3 encoders: Qwen2.5-Coder-3B (decoder-only, pos_sim=0.9855), StarCoder2-3B (decoder-only, pos_sim=0.7985), UniXcoder-base (encoder-only, 125M)\n- Training: LoRA rank=16, InfoNCE τ=0.07, batch 32, pool=8, up to 3 epochs, early stopping by val R@1, seeds {42,43,44}\n- CodeQL pipeline: 0% timeout, 78% connected-not-gold rate, 123/131 repo coverage\n\n**Key Tables**:\n\nTable 1 (Qwen R@1, pool=8, 3 seeds):\n| Strategy | Mean±Std | Δ vs Random | 95% CI |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Random | 0.570±0.027 | — | — |\n| Embedding | 0.568±0.023 | −0.17pp | [−0.92, +0.58] |\n| Dataflow | 0.560±0.014 | −0.99pp | [−2.36, +0.58] |\n| BM25 | 0.570±0.023 | +0.03pp | [−0.55, +0.59] |\n\nTable 2 (Paired within-seed deltas, Qwen):\n| Comparison | s42 | s43 | s44 | Mean | 95% CI |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| embed−random | −0.37 | +0.52 | −0.67 | −0.17 | [−0.92, +0.58] |\n| dataflow−random | −0.47 | +0.10 | −2.59 | −0.99 | [−2.36, +0.58] |\n| bm25−random | +0.23 | +0.26 | −0.39 | +0.03 | [−0.55, +0.59] |\n\nTable 3 (StarCoder2-3B, pool=8, 3 seeds):\n| Strategy | Mean±Std | Δ vs Random | 95% CI |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Random | 0.480±0.017 | — | — |\n| Embedding | 0.476±0.017 | −0.37pp | [−0.52, −0.22] |\n| Dataflow | 0.471±0.016 | −0.86pp | [−1.02, −0.70] |\nNote: Per-encoder CIs EXCLUDE zero for StarCoder (mining hurts).\n\nTable 4 (UniXcoder-base 125M, pool=8, 3 seeds):\n| Strategy | Mean±Std | Δ vs Random | 95% CI |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Random | 0.564±0.012 | — | — |\n| Embedding | 0.560±0.012 | −0.39pp | [−0.85, +0.07] |\n| Dataflow | 0.561±0.012 | −0.22pp | — |\n\nTable 5 (Data scaling, embed−random, Qwen):\n| Data Size | Δ R@1 (pp) | 95% CI | Random R@1 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1K | +1.2 | [+0.1, +2.3] | 0.48 |\n| 3K | +0.5 | [−0.4, +1.4] | 0.52 |\n| 5K | +0.2 | [−0.7, +1.1] | 0.54 |\n| 11K | −0.17 | [−0.92, +0.58] | 0.570 |\n\nTable 6 (Pool size sensitivity, Qwen):\n| Pool | Random | Embed | Dataflow | Embed−Rand |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 8 | 0.570 | 0.568 | 0.560 | −0.17pp |\n| 50 | 0.420 | 0.415 | 0.408 | −0.5pp |\n| 100 | 0.310 | 0.308 | 0.303 | −0.2pp |\n| 200 | 0.210 | 0.208 | 0.205 | −0.2pp |\n| 500 | 0.115 | 0.113 | 0.111 | −0.2pp |\n\nTable 7 (RepoBench-R, Qwen, 3 seeds):\n| Strategy | Easy (pool~6) | Hard (pool~18) | Δ |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Random | 0.875±0.015 | 0.510±0.020 | 36.5pp |\n| Embedding | 0.870±0.014 | 0.507±0.019 | 36.3pp |\n| Dataflow | 0.868±0.016 | 0.503±0.021 | 36.5pp |\n\nTable 8 (Checkpoint criterion divergence): Shows R@1_best vs R@1_val_loss for 8 runs; 67% show divergence, gap up to 1.97pp, 3 ranking flips.\n\nTable 9 (Analyst flexibility decomposition):\n| Source | Variance (pp) | % |\n|---|---|---|\n| Pool size | 6.7 | 44% |\n| Seed selection | 5.0 | 33% |\n| Checkpoint criterion | 3.5 | 23% |\n| **Total** | **15.2** | **100%** |\n| Mining strategy | 0.5 | 3.3% |\n\nTable 10 (Power analysis): 3 seeds → detectable effect 2.5pp; 10 seeds → 0.8pp; 50 seeds → 0.3pp.\n\nTable 11 (Reproduction): CoCoHaNeRe +3.5→+0.2pp (CI [−0.8,+1.2]), ANCE +2.8→+0.4pp (CI [−0.5,+1.3]).\n\nTable 12 (Temperature ablation): τ∈{0.05,0.07,0.20}, all CIs include zero.\n\nTable 13 (Stratified by repo size): All strata CIs include zero.\n\nSection 4.11 additional ablations: Dynamic vs static mining (0.567 vs 0.568), hop depth 1-3 (<0.2pp), distance weighting (0.558 vs 0.560), fusion head (0.571 vs 0.569), failure analysis (8 help, 32 irrelevant, 10 hurt out of 50).\n\n**Experiment Design Context** (full planned experiments — check paper coverage):\nThe full experiment plan included 20+ experiments. Key ones that should appear:\n- M1 CodeQL extraction (covered in Sec 3.3)\n- M2 Qwen 4-strategy × 3-seed (Table 1-2)\n- SWE-bench evaluation (DROPPED — R@1=0.04 vs BM25=0.2733, OOD failure, not mentioned in paper)\n- RepoBench-R (Table 7)\n- Stratified analysis across 5 dimensions (Table 13 only shows repo size)\n- Hop depth ablation (Sec 4.11)\n- Data scaling 1K/3K/5K/11K (Table 5)\n- Fusion head (Sec 4.11)\n- Dataflow distance weighting (Sec 4.11)\n- Failure analysis 50 cases (Sec 4.11)\n- Qualitative examples 5-10 cases (NOT in paper)\n- StarCoder 3-strategy × 3-seed (Table 3, but only shows random/embed/dataflow — no BM25 for StarCoder)\n- Temperature ablation (Table 12, reduced to single seed s42)\n- Dynamic embedding mining (Sec 4.11)\n- BM25 baseline (Table 1)\n- Pool size sensitivity (Table 6)\n- UniXcoder 3-seed (Table 4, but missing CI for dataflow)\n- Extra seeds 5-10