{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01M9AL1VepzVgtubiQystuzn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01S2DnMJTdKE4eUY3uxbB7dS","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_5/projects/structfuse-context/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T150348/worst/paper.tex","limit":30},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":262,"cache_read_input_tokens":59782,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":262,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":72,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0146hp8VT5paWxaM372yiqKN","session_id":"163407e6-eb14-494f-b2b9-ca41e03f7d7e","uuid":"2501f363-72e5-4e4a-acab-acdedcbc4f29"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01S2DnMJTdKE4eUY3uxbB7dS","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{times}\n4\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{amsmath}\n7\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n8\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n9\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n10\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n11\t\\usepackage[colorlinks=true,citecolor=blue,urlcolor=blue]{hyperref}\n12\t\n13\t\\title{Checkpoint Selection Matters More Than Mining Strategy:\\\\A Negative Result Study on Hard Negative Mining for Code Retrieval}\n14\t\n15\t\\author{Anonymous}\n16\t\n17\t\\begin{document}\n18\t\\maketitle\n19\t\n20\t\\begin{abstract}\n21\tHard negative mining is widely assumed to improve contrastive code retrieval, yet reported gains are rarely accompanied by multi-seed confidence intervals or controlled evaluation methodology.\n22\tWe present the most comprehensive controlled study to date: 4 mining strategies $\\times$ 3 seeds $\\times$ 3 encoder architectures (Qwen2.5-Coder-3B, StarCoder2-3B, UniXcoder-base), evaluated at multiple pool sizes (8--500) and training data scales (1K--11K instances).\n23\tOur findings are unambiguous: \\textbf{no mining strategy reliably outperforms random sampling} at training scales of 10K+ instances.\n24\tAll pairwise strategy comparisons yield 95\\% bootstrap CIs that include zero (mean absolute delta $<$0.5pp across encoders).\n25\tCross-seed variance (2--5pp) exceeds within-seed strategy effects by 4--10$\\times$.\n26\tWe identify a far larger source of reported performance variation: checkpoint selection criterion (best-by-R@1 vs.\\ best-by-validation-loss) causes 10--18pp variation in reported results---30$\\times$ larger than any mining strategy effect.\n27\tData scaling analysis reveals that mining strategy effectiveness monotonically decreases with training corpus size, crossing zero at $\\sim$4K instances, explaining why pilot-scale gains fail to replicate.\n28\tWe reproduce two published mining papers' claims (CoCoHaNeRe, ANCE) in our controlled setting and find neither replicates with proper multi-seed CIs.\n29\tWe propose a minimum evaluation protocol for mining strategy research: $\\geq$5 seeds, dual checkpoint criteria reporting, and multi-pool evaluation.\n30\t\\end{abstract}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0146hp8VT5paWxaM372yiqKN","session_id":"163407e6-eb14-494f-b2b9-ca41e03f7d7e","uuid":"3d7051ab-0d7a-4380-980b-8e9499a99dbe","timestamp":"2026-05-16T16:17:33.422Z"}
