{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0146hp8VT5paWxaM372yiqKN","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is the summary:\n\n**R2 Review for \"Checkpoint Selection Matters More Than Mining Strategy\" (worst tier) --- 2/6 Reject**\n\nKey findings from the review:\n\n**Strongest element**: The analyst flexibility decomposition (checkpoint criterion effect of 3.5pp) is the paper's genuinely novel contribution, but it is under-developed and buried as a secondary finding.\n\n**Critical weaknesses identified**:\n\n1. **Predictable negative result**: \"Mining helps less when you have more data\" follows directly from contrastive learning scaling behavior (known from ANCE, SimCLR, HCL). Not sufficiently surprising for a top venue.\n\n2. **Inflated \"30x ratio\"**: The 15.2pp \"analyst flexibility\" figure bundles pool size (a task difficulty parameter) and seed variance (inherent stochasticity) with the genuine checkpoint criterion confound. The honest ratio is ~7x (3.5pp / 0.5pp), not 30x.\n\n3. **Invalid reproduction claims**: CoCoHaNeRe proposes gradient-based mining; the paper tests static embedding mining. ANCE proposes dynamic re-mining; the paper tests static mining. These are different experiments in different domains, not reproductions.\n\n4. **Only 8 references** for a self-described \"systematic study\" --- missing entire categories (contrastive learning foundations, hard negative theory, code search benchmarks, dense retrieval systems, MTEB/BEIR evaluation standards).\n\n5. **\"Scaling law\" from 4 data points** is a significant overstatement.\n\n6. **Pool=8 training confound**: Within-repo random sampling at K=7 is already semi-hard, potentially explaining the null result as a ceiling on the training signal rather than genuine mining ineffectiveness.\n\n7. **Zero figures** in a 10-page paper with 13 tables.\n\nThe review was saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_5/projects/structfuse-context/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T150348/worst/review_report.md`."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a95e65ec054d35792 (use SendMessage with to: 'a95e65ec054d35792' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 78283\ntool_uses: 22\nduration_ms: 212721</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"163407e6-eb14-494f-b2b9-ca41e03f7d7e","uuid":"3b6b2a57-7224-4360-89d1-efc03a1405f9","timestamp":"2026-05-16T16:20:18.742Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are **Reviewer 2 (R2)** for a top ML venue peer review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject … 6=Strong Accept). Your focus areas are **novelty, positioning, related work, and broader impact**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"Checkpoint Selection Matters More Than Mining Strategy: A Negative Result Study on Hard Negative Mining for Code Retrieval\"\n\n**Core Claims**:\n1. No mining strategy reliably outperforms random sampling at training scales of 10K+ instances, across 4 strategies × 3 seeds × 3 encoders.\n2. Checkpoint selection criterion (best-by-R@1 vs best-by-val-loss) causes 10–18pp variation — 30× larger than mining strategy effect.\n3. Mining effectiveness is inversely proportional to corpus size (scaling law crossing zero at ~4K instances).\n4. Reproduction of CoCoHaNeRe (+3.5pp→+0.2pp) and ANCE (+2.8pp→+0.4pp) fails under controlled conditions.\n5. Proposed minimum evaluation protocol: ≥5 seeds, dual checkpoint criteria, multi-pool evaluation.\n\n**Paper Structure** (10 pages):\n- Section 1 Introduction: Motivates the study, lists 5 contributions\n- Section 2 Related Work (short, ~0.5 page): 3 paragraphs covering (a) Hard Negative Mining for Dense Retrieval (ANCE, DPR, curriculum), (b) Code Retrieval and Mining (CoCoHaNeRe, SweRank, GraphCodeBERT), (c) Evaluation Methodology in Retrieval (sentence embedding pitfalls)\n- Section 3 Methodology: Task (SWE-smith file-level retrieval), 4 strategies, CodeQL pipeline, training config (LoRA rank=16, τ=0.07, pool=8), 3 encoders\n- Section 4 Experiments (bulk of paper): Main results, cross-architecture, data scaling, pool size, RepoBench-R, checkpoint criterion, power analysis, reproduction, temperature ablation, stratified analysis, additional ablations\n- Section 5 Discussion: Why mining fails at scale, representation geometry, implications\n- Section 6 Conclusion\n- References: 8 citations total\n\n**Key experimental results** (all from tables):\n- Qwen: All 4 strategies within noise (Table 1), sign-inconsistent across seeds (Table 2)\n- StarCoder: Mining consistently HURTS (CIs exclude zero per-encoder, Table 3)\n- UniXcoder: Null, <0.4pp range per seed (Table 4)\n- Data scaling: embed−random delta goes from +1.2pp (1K) to −0.17pp (11K) monotonically (Table 5)\n- Pool size: 8→500 causes 45.5pp R@1 drop, strategy delta stays <0.5pp (Table 6)\n- RepoBench-R: Pool effect (36pp) >> strategy effect (<1pp) (Table 7)\n- Checkpoint criterion: Up to 1.97pp gap per run, 67% divergence, 3 ranking flips (Table 8)\n- Analyst flexibility: 15.2pp total methodology variance vs 0.5pp strategy effect (Table 9)\n- Power analysis: 3 seeds can only detect ≥2.5pp effects (Table 10)\n- Reproduction: Neither CoCoHaNeRe nor ANCE replicates (Table 11)\n- Temperature: Null across τ∈{0.05,0.07,0.20} (Table 12)\n- Stratified: No subgroup significant (Table 13)\n- Additional ablations in Sec 4.11: dynamic mining, hop depth, distance weighting, fusion head, failure analysis\n\n**Experiment Design Context** (what was planned vs delivered):\nThe full experimental plan included 20+ experiments. The paper covers most of them but:\n- SWE-bench evaluation was dropped (R@1=0.04 vs BM25 0.2733 — OOD failure, not mentioned)\n- Qualitative examples (5-10 cases) not included\n- Stratified analysis only shows repo size (plan had 5 dimensions: repo size, pool size, query-pos similarity, structural data availability, neg_difficulty quartile)\n- StarCoder results don't include BM25 strategy\n- UniXcoder missing CI for dataflow\n- Many individual run results are predicted/imagined rather than actually computed\n- No figures in the paper (plan described ~12 figures)\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nWrite a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, focus on novelty and positioning)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, focus on novelty, related work coverage, and framing)\n4. **Questions for Authors** (numbered)\n5. **Novelty Assessment** (detailed paragraph)\n6. **Related Work Gaps** (specific missing citations/areas)\n7. **Minor Issues**\n8. **Overall Assessment** with scores:\n   - Soundness (1-5)\n   - Presentation (1-5)\n   - Contribution (1-5)\n   - Overall (1-6, where 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept)\n   - Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous. Key things to scrutinize from your perspective:\n\n**Novelty concerns:**\n- \"Negative result\" papers have lower novelty bar but must provide surprising or actionable insights. Is \"mining doesn't help at scale\" genuinely surprising, or is it predictable from known scaling behavior of contrastive learning?\n- The checkpoint criterion finding is potentially novel — but is it well-developed enough? Table 8 only shows 8 runs (not the full 4×3×3=36 matrix). The \"15.2pp analyst flexibility\" (Table 9) adds pool_size + seed + checkpoint — but pool size and seed effects are already known; what's the incremental insight?\n- The data scaling \"law\" (Table 5) has only 4 data points. Calling it a \"scaling law\" is a stretch.\n- Proposed evaluation protocol (≥5 seeds, dual checkpoint, multi-pool) — how much of this is already standard practice? MTEB and similar benchmarks already use multiple seeds.\n\n**Related work gaps:**\n- Only 8 references is extremely thin for a top venue paper\n- Missing: Sentence-BERT/MTEB evaluation standards, ColBERT, Contriever, E5, GTE — major dense retrieval works\n- Missing: Mining strategy work in NLP beyond code (e.g., TAS-B, SimANS, ADORE)\n- Missing: Negative result papers in ML (is there a venue tradition? TMLR? NeurIPS Datasets?)\n- Missing: Hard negative mining theory (e.g., \"what makes negatives hard\" by Kalantidis et al., curriculum learning theory)\n- Missing: Code search benchmarks (CodeSearchNet, CoSQA, besides RepoBench-R)\n- No comparison with gradient-based mining (CoCoHaNeRe's main claim) — only non-gradient methods tested\n- The paper dismisses CoCoHaNeRe but doesn't test CoCoHaNeRe's actual method\n\n**Framing concerns:**\n- Title says \"Checkpoint Selection Matters More\" but checkpoint analysis is secondary (Tables 8-9 only)\n- The paper tries to be both \"negative result study\" AND \"methodological audit\" — are these coherent?\n- \"Most comprehensive controlled study\" claim — is it? Only 3 seeds base, single task (file-level retrieval), single dataset (SWE-smith), LoRA-only\n- Scope is very narrow: file-level code retrieval from SWE-smith. How generalizable are the findings?\n\n**Presentation concerns:**\n- No figures at all — unusual for a 10-page paper\n- 13 tables is very dense\n- Related work section is too short (~0.5 page)\n\nOutput your complete review in structured markdown.","agentId":"a95e65ec054d35792","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is the summary:\n\n**R2 Review for \"Checkpoint Selection Matters More Than Mining Strategy\" (worst tier) --- 2/6 Reject**\n\nKey findings from the review:\n\n**Strongest element**: The analyst flexibility decomposition (checkpoint criterion effect of 3.5pp) is the paper's genuinely novel contribution, but it is under-developed and buried as a secondary finding.\n\n**Critical weaknesses identified**:\n\n1. **Predictable negative result**: \"Mining helps less when you have more data\" follows directly from contrastive learning scaling behavior (known from ANCE, SimCLR, HCL). Not sufficiently surprising for a top venue.\n\n2. **Inflated \"30x ratio\"**: The 15.2pp \"analyst flexibility\" figure bundles pool size (a task difficulty parameter) and seed variance (inherent stochasticity) with the genuine checkpoint criterion confound. The honest ratio is ~7x (3.5pp / 0.5pp), not 30x.\n\n3. **Invalid reproduction claims**: CoCoHaNeRe proposes gradient-based mining; the paper tests static embedding mining. ANCE proposes dynamic re-mining; the paper tests static mining. These are different experiments in different domains, not reproductions.\n\n4. **Only 8 references** for a self-described \"systematic study\" --- missing entire categories (contrastive learning foundations, hard negative theory, code search benchmarks, dense retrieval systems, MTEB/BEIR evaluation standards).\n\n5. **\"Scaling law\" from 4 data points** is a significant overstatement.\n\n6. **Pool=8 training confound**: Within-repo random sampling at K=7 is already semi-hard, potentially explaining the null result as a ceiling on the training signal rather than genuine mining ineffectiveness.\n\n7. **Zero figures** in a 10-page paper with 13 tables.\n\nThe review was saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_5/projects/structfuse-context/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T150348/worst/review_report.md`."}],"totalDurationMs":212721,"totalTokens":78283,"totalToolUseCount":22,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5740,"cache_read_input_tokens":72051,"output_tokens":491,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5740},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":491,"cache_read_input_tokens":72051,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5740,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5740,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":9,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":10}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01XzbVtfnZ2FVghomJNacXGG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Both reviewers have completed their independent reviews. Let me now proceed to the roundtable process.\n\nLet me summarize what I got:\n\n**R1 (Methods + Experiments)**: Score 2/6 (Reject)\nKey concerns:\n1. Insufficient seeds (3 vs own recommendation of 50)\n2. Many results appear predicted/imputed not actually run\n3. Pool=8 as primary eval is too easy\n4. Dataflow 66% fallback dilutes comparison\n5. Bootstrap CI methodology unclear (instance vs cluster)\n6. \"Analyst flexibility\" decomposition misleading\n\n\n7. Reproduction claims weakly grounded (different tasks/settings)\n8. Only LoRA fine-tuning tested\n9. No figures\n10. Training regime inconsistency across seeds\n11. In-training eval noise floor ~0.5pp\n\n**R2 (Novelty + Related Work)**: Score 2/6 (Reject)\nKey concerns:\n1. Predictable negative result (not surprising)\n2. Inflated \"30x ratio\" — honest ratio is ~7x\n3. Invalid reproduction claims (different methods tested)\n4. Only 8 references — extremely thin\n5. \"Scaling law\" from 4 data points\n6. Pool=8 training conf\n\nBoth reviewers are aligned on fundamental issues: the experimental setup is underpowered with insufficient seeds and questionable evaluation choices, the reproduction claims don't hold up under scrutiny, and the novelty is limited with sparse related work coverage and an inflated framing of the results.\n\nNow I'm moving into the cross-review synthesis phase to identify where the reviewers converge and where they diverge, then consolidate this into a final assessment.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10115,"cache_read_input_tokens":74626,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10115},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","i