{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01TSv2rWAUNTC2mKt9LWTTf6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me now do the roundtable Steps 2-3 (cross-sharing and convergence), then produce the final report.\n\n## Step 2: Cross-Sharing Summary\n\n### R1's unique concerns (not raised by R2):\n1. Training regime inconsistency across seeds (s42 used 3 epochs/patience=0 vs s43/s44 2 epochs/patience=2)\n2. In-training eval noise floor from random negative re-sampling (~0.5pp)\n3. Bootstrap CI methodology (naive vs cluster)\n4. K=7 (number of negatives) ablation missing\n\n\n5. Dynamic mining needs multi-seed validation\n6. SWE-bench failure rates weren't reported as part of the downstream task evaluation\n7. Pool=8 appears artificially easy with supporting evidence\n\n### R2's unique concerns (not raised by R1):\n1. Novelty: negative result is predictable from known scaling behavior\n2. The honest ratio is ~7x not 30x (after stripping pool size and seed which are known confounds)\n3. Pool=8 training creates semi-hard negatives by within-repo sampling (different angle from R1's pool=8 eval critique)\n4. Related work is extremely thin (8 references, missing entire categories)\n5. No gradient-based mining tested (CoCoHaNeRe's actual method)\n6. Paper doesn't meet its own proposed evaluation standard\n7. \"Scaling law\" terminology from 4 data points\n8. Scope concern: single task, single dataset\n\n### Shared concerns (consensus):\nBoth reviewers flag that three seeds fall short of what the paper itself requires, and both worry the results might be imputed rather than empirically derived. The pool=8 limitation troubles them from different angles—R1 sees it as an evaluation problem while R2 views it as a training issue that creates artificially easy negatives. They both note the unfair Dataflow 66% fallback comparison, question the validity of reproduction claims given different tasks and methods, and point out the missing figures and full fine-tuning experiments beyond LoRA.\n\nThe checkpoint criterion finding stands out as genuinely interesting to both, though underdeveloped.\n\n### Disagreements:\nThere aren't any major ones—both converge on Reject (2/6), just emphasizing different failure modes. R1 leans into statistical and methodological problems while R2 focuses on novelty and framing issues.\n\n## Step 3: Convergence\n\nThe panel clearly recommends rejection. The core problems fall into three buckets: the paper's own power analysis proves three seeds can't detect the effects it's studying, yet it draws conclusions anyway; a significant portion of results appear predicted rather than measured without disclosure; and the experimental setup creates unfair comparisons that undermine the claims.\n\nThat said, the checkpoint criterion work and the data scaling trends are worth developing further. With more seeds, completed experiments, transparent reporting, and better positioning against related work, this could become a solid paper. The evaluation protocol guidance is also sound.\n\nNow I'm synthesizing this into the final report.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1046,"cache_read_input_tokens":84741,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1046},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"163407e6-eb14-494f-b2b9-ca41e03f7d7e","uuid":"f323115d-61ac-49c6-8cb4-9bdcb9d8bad3"}
